 ## Overview
-Opacity regularization is a technique used in differentiable volume rendering and radiance-field optimization to prevent front splats (e.g., Gaussians) from saturating too early during training [S2]. By penalizing high mean opacity and opacity logits that exceed a prescribed threshold, it ensures that gradient flow reaches deeper parts of the representation, which is critical for compact scene representations where a limited number of primitives must explain both visible and hidden structure [S2]. It is a specific instance of [Masked Opacity Regularization](11046-masked-opacity-regularization.md), distinguished by its application to the global mean and logit distribution rather than spatial masks.
